Exact-head update: `34f5050d08ad39a7b630756fe2d8118f4c8e969e` (base `aff5125f8edf5095aef5d3d79bbbb101c95b9413`; effective `AGENTS.md` hash `1a804b27143e4d9b9fc80b4497e6cd05a78a214db2c8195fb4d0504ebaf588f3`).

Additional fixes on this head:

- one global admission boundary now covers normal turns, API runs, background turns, and cron jobs;
- expensive browser/Chrome and CUA process trees now join independently bounded worker scopes;
- bounded defaults are 3 parallel / 2048 MiB headroom / 16 queued;
- diagnostics report per-worker cgroup memory and OOM events;
- the repository includes the documented alert-only replacement for the restart-on-lock host guard.

Exact-tree verification: 289 passed, 6 platform skips, 0 failed; Ruff and `git diff --check` clean. Controlled 6-worker stress peaked at 3 active, emitted 3 queue notices, had zero worker failures, and kept the parent/gateway test process alive.

Read-only VM proof after the tests: live gateway PID `4179876`, `NRestarts=0`, service cgroup `oom=0`, `oom_kill=0`. The new guard was dry-run only with `/tmp` state and recorded `recovery_attempted=false`; no live code/config/unit/process was changed.

Maintainer exact-head review and required gates are requested. The PR remains `mergeStateStatus=BLOCKED` with no reviews. CI, Docker Build, and Nix flake were dispatched for this exact head but each concluded `action_required` with zero jobs, so maintainer workflow approval is also required. The PR has not been merged or deployed. Live Telegram concurrency/queue/failure acceptance remains a post-merge controlled-window gate.

@codex review

Please review exact current head `2ce15b344010ab37ba706194ddc2d3419a3c6025` after rebase onto `main` `9d9f44d63826b18503f44c754e48e1f4f83b3a6e`.

Fresh exact-head evidence: 279 passed, 6 platform skips, 0 failed across all 13 changed test files with the pinned Python 3.11 environment and Slack extra; Ruff and `git diff --check` clean. Controlled stress: 6 workers, limit 3, peak active 3, 3 queue notices, one deliberate worker crash isolated, queue resumed/drained, and control-plane process survived. No production process or service was modified.

Re-reviewed exact head `77ba9c8bc0b8e1903534735c0b2354ad49235f9f` against live `main@ac6c8028e00d01ee2f299ba7fd03329c7f10382d`. I re-read the actual five-file diff, the shared holder scanner, the repair-admission call site, the argv classifier, the new Linux failure witnesses, and the integrated #92419 / #96011 lineage rather than carrying forward the first-head verdict.

The first review's **detector correctness and attribution findings are closed on this object**:

- readable descriptors are no longer trusted by pathname alone; `(st_dev, st_ino)` is authoritative for live files, and the same-device fallback is restricted to genuinely deleted DB/WAL/SHM targets;
- non-disappearance `readlink(fd)` / `stat(fd)` failures produce an unknown descriptor and block repair before SQLite is opened;
- uninspectable processes are classified from argv execution identity rather than substring presence, with positive coverage for the supported Hermes executable/module/script entrypoints and negative coverage for wrappers, log readers, unrelated modules, `-c`, and argument-only mentions;
- the same helper now feeds both top-level repair admission and the existing FTS-maintenance path;
- the contributor mappings are present without rewriting the cherry-picked authorship, and the PR body now preserves the #90871 → #91839, #93191/#93453, #92419, and #96011 lineage.

I do not see a new source-level defect in those repaired semantics. The live same-path/different-inode witness covers both same-device and different-device peers, the deleted-WAL subprocess remains a real inode-generation witness, and the repair test still proves refusal occurs before `_connect_repair_durable`.

One repository ownership blocker remains, and it is now the only code-shape blocker I am carrying forward: **this still grows `hermes_state.py` instead of landing the new reusable holder/repair authority behind the active shard graph.** Live #78636 is explicit that `hermes_state.py` is a god file under the repo-wide “all god files are sharded, never reverted” policy and that the correct end state is the full shard, not new long-term behavior in the monolith. The fact that current `main` does not yet contain a canonical state-repair module is not evidence that the monolith is the right new owner; it means the new boundary has to be coordinated with that decomposition rather than becoming another reason the extraction can never finish.

The bounded seam is already visible in this patch: `_foreign_state_db_holders()` plus argv/descriptor identity and repair-admission semantics form one coherent owner, while `SessionDB._foreign_state_db_holders()` can remain a compatibility delegate. Please land that authority through the #78636/#78647 decomposition graph (or restack onto the canonical shard once its owner is established) rather than making the 9K+ file the permanent implementation location.

The release gate is independently unsatisfied. Exact-head CI `33218729756`, Docker `33218729239`, and Nix `33218729309` all concluded `action_required`; there is no hosted execution receipt for this exact object. This PR now has **seven surviving commits**, so eventual acceptance also requires green hosted evidence for every one of those surviving objects, not just a later green top commit.

So the substantive state is narrower than on the first pass: the identity/false-positive/lineage repair is good and no longer blocking; the remaining work is to put that now-correct authority behind the repository's actual state shard boundary and then produce the seven-commit exact-object green chain (or rematerialize the reviewed tree as a smaller coherent current-main train and green every surviving commit).
